@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Transactions Introduction";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">
            Transactions Introduction
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted fs-5">
            Learn the basics of SQL Server Transactions,
            why transactions are used, and how COMMIT,
            ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT work.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS A TRANSACTION?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Transaction?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Transaction</strong> is a group of one or more
                SQL statements that are treated as a single unit of work.
            </p>

            <p>
                A transaction allows us to make sure that a group of
                database operations is completed successfully.
                If something goes wrong, the changes can be rolled back.
            </p>

            <p>
                Transactions are commonly used when multiple database
                operations must be performed together.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY TRANSACTIONS?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why Do We Use Transactions?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Transactions help maintain the consistency and reliability
                of data in a database.
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To group multiple SQL operations together.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To save changes only when all required operations
                    are successful.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To undo changes when an error occurs.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To maintain database consistency.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To control when changes become permanent.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Real-World Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider a bank transfer from Account A to Account B.
            </p>

            <p>
                Two operations are required:
            </p>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Deduct money from Account A.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Add money to Account B.
                </li>

            </ol>

            <p>
                Both operations should succeed together.
                If money is deducted from Account A but the second
                operation fails, the first operation should also be
                undone.
            </p>

            <div class="alert alert-info">

                <strong>Transaction Concept:</strong>

                <br />

                Either all required operations are completed successfully,
                or the changes can be rolled back.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC TRANSACTION SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Transaction Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- SQL statements

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <strong>BEGIN TRANSACTION</strong> starts the
                transaction and <strong>COMMIT</strong> makes the
                changes permanent.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BEGIN TRANSACTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                BEGIN TRANSACTION
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>BEGIN TRANSACTION</strong> starts a new
                transaction.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                After the transaction starts, SQL statements executed
                as part of that transaction can be committed or rolled back.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMIT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                COMMIT
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>COMMIT</strong> permanently saves the changes
                made during the transaction.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <br />

                If all operations are successful, use
                <strong>COMMIT</strong> to save the changes.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ROLLBACK
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                ROLLBACK
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ROLLBACK</strong> is used to undo changes
                made during a transaction that have not been committed.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ROLLBACK;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <br />

                If an error occurs during the transaction,
                we can use <strong>ROLLBACK</strong> to undo
                the transaction's changes.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TRANSACTION EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple Transaction Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Let's create a simple transaction using a Students table.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    105,
    'Amit',
    'Pune',
    23
);

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The INSERT operation is performed inside the transaction.
                When COMMIT is executed, the change becomes permanent.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ROLLBACK EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                ROLLBACK Example
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    106,
    'Sneha',
    'Mumbai',
    22
);

ROLLBACK;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In this example, the INSERT operation is undone because
                the transaction is rolled back.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TRANSACTION FLOW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Transaction Flow
            </h2>

            <div class="alert alert-primary">

                <strong>Transaction Flow:</strong>

                <br /><br />

                BEGIN TRANSACTION

                <br />

                ↓

                <br />

                SQL Statements

                <br />

                ↓

                <br />

                Check Result

                <br />

                ↓

                <br />

                <strong>COMMIT</strong>
                &nbsp; → &nbsp;
                Save Changes

                <br />

                OR

                <br />

                <strong>ROLLBACK</strong>
                &nbsp; → &nbsp;
                Undo Changes

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TRANSACTION STATES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Transaction Commands
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>

                            <th>Command</th>

                            <th>Purpose</th>

                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <code>BEGIN TRANSACTION</code>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Starts a transaction.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <code>COMMIT</code>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Permanently saves the transaction changes.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <code>ROLLBACK</code>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Undoes uncommitted transaction changes.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <code>SAVE TRANSACTION</code>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Creates a savepoint inside a transaction.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A transaction groups multiple SQL operations into
                    a single unit of work.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>BEGIN TRANSACTION</strong> starts the
                    transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>COMMIT</strong> saves the changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>ROLLBACK</strong> undoes uncommitted changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong> creates a
                    savepoint inside a transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Transactions help maintain data consistency.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            A transaction is a group of SQL statements treated as
            one unit of work.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides commands such as
            <strong>BEGIN TRANSACTION</strong>,
            <strong>COMMIT</strong>,
            <strong>ROLLBACK</strong> and
            <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong>
            to control database changes.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Transactions are useful when multiple database operations
            need to be completed reliably and consistently.
        </p>

    </div>


